.\" Distro-authored man page for the Fedora pidp8i package.
.\" Candidate for upstreaming to the PiDP-8/I project.
.TH OS8-CP 1 "November 21, 2026" "PiDP-8/I" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
os8-cp \- copy files between the host and an OS/8 disk image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B os8-cp
.RI [ \-dhvq "] [" "\-<dev><unit>[s]" " image" ] ...
.RI [ [ \-abiyz "] [" src ] ...
.I dest
.br
.B os8-cp
.RI [ \-dhvq "] [" \-\-action-file " action-file" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B os8-cp
boots an OS/8 environment in the simulator and then behaves like the
POSIX
.BR cp (1)
it is named after, copying files between the host filesystem and the
OS/8 environment under program control.
.PP
The copy direction follows from the arguments: a file specification
with a colon in it (for example
.IR DTA0:PROG.PA )
is an OS/8 file, everything else is a host path.
A single
.I src
with a plain
.I dest
names a host file; with several
.IR src s,
.I dest
is a host directory or an OS/8 device name.
.PP
Device images are attached with
.BI \- dev unit
pairs:
.I dev
is one of
.BR rk ,
.BR td ,
.B dt
or
.B rx
(the SIMH PDP-8 devices), and
.I unit
a unit number valid in both SIMH and the booted OS/8 system. Appending
an
.RB s
(for example
.BR \-rk0s )
designates that image as the system image to boot; exactly one system
image may be so marked, and it must contain a working boot block.
.PP
Remember that OS/8 writes to the
.B SYS:
and
.B DSK:
RAM-disk devices are lost when the simulator exits: those copies are
for the session, not durable storage.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a \-b \-i \-y \-z
copy actions and modes described in the verbose usage message
.RB ( "\-v \-h" );
.B \-z
zeroes pre-existing images before attaching.
.TP
.BI \-\-action-file " action-file"
take the operation list from a file; overrides earlier device and file
arguments on the command line.
.TP
.B \-d\fR,\ \-v\fR,\ \-q\fR,\ \-h
debug, verbose, quiet, usage
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ os8-cp \-rk0s os8v3d-patched.rk05 \-dt0 scratch.tu56 \-a prog.pa DTA0:PROG.PA
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR os8-run (1),
.BR os8pkg (1),
.BR cp (1)
